window.deepArticles = window.deepArticles || {};

const cadA = "<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>";
const cadE = "<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>";
const cadP = "<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup>";
const cadLink = (slug,label) => `<a href='${slug}.html'>${label}</a>`;
const cadTable = (heads,rows) => `<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr>${heads.map(h=>`<th>${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map(v=>`<td>${v}</td>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</tbody></table></div>`;
const cadArticle = c => ({category:"Civil aircraft development",eyebrow:"Civil aircraft · 1941–2012",infoboxKicker:"Aircraft and industrial history",...c,sources:[{href:"../materialCultureReference%20-%20AltWWII.md",label:"Material culture and civilian industry"},{href:"../settingBible%20-%20AltWWII.md",label:"Setting Bible"}],categories:c.categories||["Civil aviation","American aircraft industry","Aircraft development"]});

Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "cessna":cadArticle({
    title:"Cessna",eyebrow:"United States · personal transport, utility aircraft and business jets",turns:"004–010,020–028,034–040,052–078,098–104,110–118",
    lead:`Cessna is an American manufacturer of training, personal, utility and business aircraft. Its light-aircraft range linked inexpensive training to family transport, rough-field operations and fast business twins. The company later developed the Citation jet family. The Open Door Depression reduced its manufacturing base while leaving Cessna active in business aviation.${cadE}`,
    canon:"The manufacturer and its continuing civil role are established. Detailed alternate model milestones are incorporated transcript developments; historical production totals, historical corporate acquisitions, and an exact post-crash asset schedule are not inherited automatically.",
    sections:[
      {id:"light-aircraft",title:"Light-aircraft production",html:`<p>The 120/140 family gave Cessna a two-seat cabin aircraft above the cheapest fabric-covered trainers. The four-place 170 expanded its transportation market. Tricycle undercarriage widened the appeal of the subsequent 172 to customers who had learned at local fields rather than in military service. Dealers, instructors, mechanics and standardized parts supported the range.${cadE}</p>${cadTable(["Period","Aircraft or development","Role and milestone"],[["Mid-1940s","120/140 family","Two-seat cabin aircraft; earlier civilian development than a wartime production schedule required."],["1952–53","170B and 180","170B refinement; 180 prototype in 1952 and production in 1953 in the development sequence."],["1953–54","310","Fast business twin; prototype followed by certification and production in 1954."],["1955–56","172","Tricycle-gear prototype and initial production followed by a 1956-model market introduction."],["Late 1950s–1960","150, 175, 182 and 210","Training, intermediate and higher-performance singles broadened the range; the retractable 210 joined the range in 1960."],["1965","337 Skymaster","Centerline-thrust twin with a push-pull installation; a separate operational answer from the 310."]].map(r=>[...r.slice(0,2),r[2]+cadE]))}`},
      {id:"utility",title:"Rough-field and regional aircraft",html:`<p>The 180 and its heavier utility successors served ranches, small airfields, surveying and back-country transport. They remained distinct from the nosewheel family aircraft and business twins. Limited Philippine liaison and utility use supplied operating experience; large military orders did not account for the whole civilian market.${cadE}</p><p>Cessna also studied a high-wing regional owner jet, provisionally identified as Model 515 Skyjet. Shown as a development project in 1973, it reached a first-flight stage in the 1974 development chronology. High-mounted engines, short landing gear and cabin access addressed municipal-airport operations. It was a test of a different jet configuration, not a promise that turbine aircraft could use every gravel strip served by piston Cessnas.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"citation",title:"Citation development",html:`<p>The FanJet/Citation program emphasized moderate landing speed, simpler operation, quieter propulsion and access to smaller airports. Its 1969 prototype and 1971 certification formed separate milestones. Citation entered a market already demonstrated by ${cadLink("mooney-aircraft","Mooney")}'s owner-flown jets, with a larger dealer and service organization.${cadE}</p><p>Later Citation developments increased cabin space, range and cruise performance. After the crash Cessna concentrated on light jets and business aviation. The CitationJet appeared in the 1991 program; the fast Citation X was in flight test in the mid-1990s. Exact alternate certificates, delivery totals and national speed records remain outside this model chronology.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"restructuring",title:"Post-crash restructuring",html:`<p>Cessna sold volume-production capacity, tooling and selected civil assets during the industrial reorganization following the 1990 crash. Frontier's conventional-aircraft business acquired part of that capacity. Cessna retained its name and a reduced business built around jets, engineering and support. The transfers did not turn the surviving Cessna company into Frontier Orbital Lines.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"general-aviation-manufacturers.html",label:"General-Aviation Manufacturers",kicker:"Industry"},{href:"personal-jet-aircraft.html",label:"Personal Jet Aircraft",kicker:"Jet programs"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines",kicker:"Distinct orbital carrier"}],facts:[["Principal markets","Training · family transport · utility · business"],["Light singles","120/140 · 170 · 172 · 180 · 182 · 210"],["Conventional twin","310"],["Centerline-thrust twin","337 Skymaster"],["Jet family","Citation"],["High-wing jet","Model 515 Skyjet development"],["1990s position","Reduced company; jets and support retained"]]
  }),
  "piper-aircraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Piper Aircraft",eyebrow:"United States · light aircraft, training and rural utility",turns:"004–010,014,020–028,030,034–040,052–072,102–104",
    lead:`Piper Aircraft is an American light-aircraft manufacturer whose Cub, Super Cub, Pacer and later all-metal aircraft served training, private ownership and rural transport. Its lower-cost aircraft remained part of a broad manufacturing field alongside Cessna, Aeronca, Taylorcraft and other specialists.${cadE}`,
    canon:"Piper survives as a distinct manufacturer. Its indigenous twin must remain separate from Stinson's design lineage. Exact alternate postwar certificates, ownership transactions and production totals remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"cub",title:"Cub and utility aircraft",html:`<p>The J-3 Cub supplied the pre-divergence basis for inexpensive training and private flying.${cadP} Continued civilian development produced the PA-11 and Super Cruiser school of machines, followed by the Super Cub as a working rural and rough-field aircraft. The company relied on gradual civilian sales and modest procurement rather than a wartime flood of trainer orders.${cadE}</p><p>The Super Cub served a different customer from a fast retractable aircraft. Low-speed handling, straightforward maintenance and operation from small fields mattered to ranchers, instructors and utility operators. Military liaison use in the Philippines reinforced that reputation without making every civilian machine a former service aircraft.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"cabin",title:"Pacer, Tri-Pacer and all-metal singles",html:`<p>The Pacer and nosewheel Tri-Pacer extended Piper's four-place cabin range. The Tri-Pacer served owners seeking easier ground handling; the tailwheel Pacer continued in a smaller utility market. The two configurations coexisted rather than requiring all customers to adopt the same undercarriage.${cadE}</p><p>The Comanche entered the 1958 account as Piper's all-metal, retractable-gear high-performance single. Cherokee development supplied the more accessible low-wing family and training branch. Both complemented the rough-field aircraft instead of replacing their work.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"twins",title:"Independent light-twin program",html:`<p>Piper did not acquire Stinson's Twin Stinson design. It began an indigenous light-twin study in the early 1950s, developed a prototype in the middle of the decade, and reached limited production in the later 1950s development sequence. A larger six-place derivative followed in the 1960 development sequence.${cadE}</p><p>The program aimed below the cost and cabin prestige of Beech and Aero Commander. The Piper twin retained its own design identity and did not receive an inherited Stinson model name. Stinson's Twin Stinson, Twin Wagon and Piper's own twin are separate development entries.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"crash",title:"Restructuring after 1990",html:`<p>The Open Door Depression forced Piper to reduce its industrial base and dispose of selected production assets. Frontier acquired some former Piper tooling and capacity while Piper continued as a smaller manufacturer associated with affordable personal and training aircraft. Individual plant transfers, equity percentages and sale dates are not specified in the surviving company account.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"cessna.html",label:"Cessna",kicker:"Personal-aircraft competitor"},{href:"stinson-aircraft.html",label:"Stinson Aircraft",kicker:"Separate design lineage"},{href:"general-aviation-manufacturers.html",label:"General-Aviation Manufacturers",kicker:"Industry"}],facts:[["Principal business","Light personal and training aircraft"],["Rough-field line","Cub · Super Cub · tailwheel Pacer"],["Nosewheel cabin line","Tri-Pacer"],["All-metal development","Comanche · Cherokee"],["Twin lineage","Piper's own development; separate from Twin Stinson"],["Post-crash outcome","Smaller continuing manufacturer"]]
  }),
  "beechcraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Beechcraft",eyebrow:"United States · premium personal and executive aircraft",turns:"004–008,020–028,034–044,052–062,094–104,114",
    lead:`Beechcraft is an American manufacturer of premium personal aircraft, executive twins and turboprops. Bonanza and Twin Bonanza established its postwar market. Turbine development and the composite Starship broadened its range.${cadE} Beech and Mooney merged in 1991 while retaining their own marques.${cadA}`,
    canon:"The 1991 merger is author-established. Earlier model, prototype and turbine sequences are incorporated developments. The exact merged legal name and Frontier stake size remain provisional; historical certificates and later real-world corporate acquisitions are not automatically setting history.",
    sections:[
      {id:"range",title:"Personal and business aircraft",html:`<p>Bonanza served prosperous owner-pilots and businesses seeking greater speed and finish than the lowest-priced light aircraft. Twin Bonanza supplied a larger twin-engine executive aircraft. Beech also developed the smaller Travel Air/Baron branch between those market levels.${cadE}</p><p>The conventional-tail Debonair broadened the single-engine range around 1960. A buyer could obtain Beech engineering without the complete equipment and trim of the premium Bonanza. Beech's aircraft remained separate from the utility designs of Stinson and the lower-cost training lines of Piper.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"turbines",title:"Turboprop development",html:`<p>Beech's early turbine studies centered on six-to-eight-seat executive transport. A propeller driven by a small turbine offered a nearer-term business aircraft than the smallest personal turbojets. Engine supply, price, maintenance and certification constrained development alongside airframe design.${cadE}</p><p>The Model 87 turbine installation belonged to the experimental 1962 program. The subsequent King Air school turned that work into a more practical executive turboprop range in the middle 1960s account. Aero Commander remained a competing source of high-wing turbine twins. Experimental installation, commercial introduction and large order books are separate stages.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"starship",title:"Starship",html:`<p>The Starship introduced a composite airframe, forward control surface, rearward-facing turboprop propellers and an electronic cockpit into Beech's executive-aircraft work. Its development and demonstration were prominent in the middle and later 1980s. ERCO's earlier canards and composite research had already given American pilots experience with unconventional aircraft layouts.${cadE}</p><p>The record supports continued Starship development and customer aircraft by the end of the decade. The certifying office and exact approval instrument remain unspecified. Piaggio offered a competing layout and market approach; the comparison does not imply common ownership.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"merger",title:"Crisis and merger",html:`<p>The crash reduced Beech's access to credit and the value of its business. It disposed of selected manufacturing assets and accepted domestic capital, including a Frontier minority interest in the restructuring record.${cadE} Beech and Mooney combined in 1991, retaining their separate identities and design programs.${cadA}</p><p>The combined ${cadLink("mooney-beech-aircraft","Mooney–Beech")} organization shared purchasing, financing, engineering resources and support. Its later six-seat touring demonstrator drew upon Beech cabin and pressurization experience alongside Mooney's drag-reduction work.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"mooney-beech-aircraft.html",label:"Mooney–Beech Aircraft",kicker:"1991 combination"},{href:"mooney-aircraft.html",label:"Mooney Aircraft",kicker:"Merger partner"},{href:"aero-commander.html",label:"Aero Commander",kicker:"Turboprop competitor"},{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"Model 87 and composite development"}],facts:[["Personal aircraft","Bonanza · Debonair"],["Piston twins","Twin Bonanza · Travel Air/Baron school"],["Turbine experiment","Model 87"],["Turboprop line","King Air school"],["Composite executive aircraft","Starship"],["Merger","Mooney · 1991"],["Beech marque","Retained"]]
  }),
  "mooney-aircraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Mooney Aircraft",eyebrow:"United States · low-drag touring aircraft and owner-flown jets",turns:"026–028,034–044,046–068,078–084,098–104,114",
    lead:`Mooney is an American personal-aircraft manufacturer associated with efficient structures, low drag and owner-flown performance. Its M20 touring aircraft preceded the MJ-1 Jet Mite experimental personal jet and a later family of small owner-flown jets.${cadE} Mooney merged with Beech in 1991 and retained its marque.${cadA}`,
    canon:"The 1991 combination is fixed by the author. MJ-1, later personal-jet variants and their development milestones are incorporated transcript designs; no historical real-world Mooney jet is being imported. Production totals, certification instruments and final ownership percentages are open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"piston",title:"Piston touring aircraft",html:`<p>The M20 program placed useful performance ahead of increasing engine power alone. M20 prototypes flew in 1954, with production following in 1955. Low drag increased speed or range even when gasoline was inexpensive, and fuel saved could become useful payload.${cadE}</p><p>Mooney remained smaller than the firms supplying complete trainer-to-business-aircraft ranges. It sold to customers who valued fast personal travel and intended to fly the aircraft themselves. It retained the M20 as a practical touring machine while exploring more demanding turbine aircraft.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"mj1",title:"MJ-1 Jet Mite",html:`<p>The MJ-1 Jet Mite reached a first-flight stage late in 1962. It was a very small personal turbojet demonstrator, within the two-to-four-place design class, rather than a conventional corporate cabin jet. A small airframe did not make the turbine, fuel system or pilot qualification inexpensive.${cadE}</p><p>Early demand exceeded the initial batch available in the 1963–64 period. Mooney refined the existing aircraft before expanding the concept into a roomier, longer-range four-seat jet. Initial flights, orders and later deliveries remained different milestones. The Jet Mite's restricted baggage and range kept it separate from the more economical M20.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"jet-family",title:"Personal-jet family",html:`<p>A four-seat prototype in the middle 1960s developed the owner-jet into a touring aircraft. Refinement emphasized range, endurance, handling and useful equipment rather than making every model a record machine. Citation dealer coverage and conventional business-jet operation gave Cessna a different advantage.${cadE}</p><p>Republic's later civil high-speed demonstrators and imported German fast aircraft put pressure on Mooney. The company pursued a transonic personal-jet challenger in the later 1980s development program. A performance claim had to specify aircraft class and conditions: large SSTs were already faster than the small private-aircraft category.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"merger",title:"The 1991 merger",html:`<p>Mooney and Beech combined after the crash while preserving their marques.${cadA} Frontier's minority stakes in the two concerns formed part of the financial background; they did not establish a complete purchase of either marque. The merged concern paired Mooney aerodynamics with Beech cabin, pressurization, turboprop and support experience.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"mooney-beech-aircraft.html",label:"Mooney–Beech Aircraft",kicker:"Later company"},{href:"personal-jet-aircraft.html",label:"Personal Jet Aircraft",kicker:"Competing designs"},{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"1962 program"}],facts:[["Piston touring family","M20"],["First small-jet development","MJ-1 Jet Mite"],["MJ-1 milestone","Late 1962 first-flight development"],["Jet market","Owner-flown personal performance"],["Later program","Four-place touring jet · transonic challenger"],["Merger","Beech · 1991"],["Mooney marque","Retained"]]
  }),
  "stinson-aircraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Stinson Aircraft",eyebrow:"United States · cabin, utility and twin-boom aircraft",turns:"024–030,034–044,052–072,078,102–104",
    lead:`Stinson is an American manufacturer of family and utility aircraft whose postwar range developed around cabin space, payload, large doors and low-speed operation. It retained the Twin Stinson lineage and later developed the all-metal Station Wagon, twin-boom Twin Wagon and Jet Wagon.${cadE}`,
    canon:"Stinson's continued design identity and absence of a Piper takeover are carried transcript developments. A late-1940s independent recapitalization is provisional; exact legal instruments, investors and closing dates remain open. Twin Stinson and Twin Wagon are different programs.",
    sections:[
      {id:"organization",title:"Organization",html:`<p>Eddie Stinson died in 1932. The company had already entered the E. L. Cord organization and later became a Vultee subsidiary in 1940.${cadP} The postwar Stinson division remained active in light cabin and utility aircraft. Gradually expanding civilian sales avoided the same stock of unsold postwar aircraft that drove its historical disposal.${cadE}</p><p>The corporate sequence separates the Stinson business from its Consolidated Vultee parent around the end of the 1940s. Whether this took the form of a management purchase, dealer recapitalization or another arrangement is unresolved. Piper did not acquire Stinson or its twin design.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"cabin",title:"Cabin and utility aircraft",html:`<p>The 108 family and its 108-4 development supplied comfortable personal and utility flying. Large loading doors, adaptable cabin space and equipment for freight, medical or rural work distinguished the range from faster business singles. A later all-metal Station Wagon flew in the 1959 development record and entered production in 1960.${cadE}</p><p>The Twin Stinson was the smaller mid-1950s light-twin program. Prototype and limited-production development preceded the larger Twin Wagon. Piper's indigenous twin had a separate development lineage.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"twin-wagon",title:"Twin Wagon",html:`<p>The 1962 Twin Wagon prototype used a central cabin, twin booms and a cargo-oriented arrangement. Access, loading space, stable operation and useful payload were principal aims. It moved from prototype work into production in the middle 1960s sequence.${cadE}</p><p>Competition from overseas utility twins reinforced the piston aircraft's place in the range. Economical maintenance and payload could matter more than a jet's speed on short sectors. Stinson continued piston utility aircraft alongside its turbine work.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"jet-wagon",title:"Jet Wagon",html:`<p>The Jet Wagon grew from a six-seat twin-boom jet study authorized in the 1966 development chronology. The 1967 demonstrator retained a central passenger or cargo cabin and emphasized regional utility. Its cabin layout and loading requirements differed from Mooney's small sporting jets.${cadE}</p><p>Later studies examined remotely supervised freight operation. A 1974 study did not establish uncrewed commercial service, unrestricted civil approval or the removal of trained operators. Weather, loading, communications and recovery from failures remained part of the operational problem.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"piper-aircraft.html",label:"Piper Aircraft",kicker:"Separate twin development"},{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"Twin Wagon prototype"},{href:"personal-jet-aircraft.html",label:"Personal Jet Aircraft",kicker:"Jet Wagon"}],facts:[["Pre-divergence parent","Vultee · from 1940"],["Personal-aircraft lineage","108 family"],["New all-metal utility aircraft","Station Wagon · 1959/1960 development sequence"],["Early twin","Twin Stinson"],["Later twin-boom aircraft","Twin Wagon"],["Jet development","Jet Wagon"],["Piper acquisition","Does not occur in the carried development"]]
  }),
  "aero-commander":cadArticle({
    title:"Aero Commander",eyebrow:"United States · high-wing executive and utility twins",turns:"020–028,034–044,052–062,102",
    lead:`Aero Commander is an American manufacturer of high-wing twin-engine business and utility aircraft. Its piston twins emphasized cabin access, loading and engine-out handling. The company became an early advocate of compact turboprop power for executive and regional aircraft.${cadE}`,
    canon:"The continued manufacturer, piston twin and turbine development roles are incorporated transcript history. Exact alternate corporate owners, renamed divisions, production totals and certificate dates are not fixed by historical company outcomes.",
    sections:[
      {id:"piston",title:"Piston twins",html:`<p>The 520 and 560 families established a business-aircraft school distinct from the sleek low-wing Cessna 310 and premium Beech twins. The high wing aided cabin access and loading. The 560 joined the 1954 development record as the company refined its piston range.${cadE}</p><p>Aero Commander addressed corporate owners and utility customers whose priorities included passengers, equipment and useful operation following an engine failure. It occupied a smaller industrial position than Beech and did not need to offer every trainer or family single itself.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"turbine",title:"Turbine development",html:`<p>The firm pressed engine suppliers to produce turbines small and economical enough for a cabin twin. It could adapt an existing aircraft to test a new powerplant rather than waiting for a complete personal-jet market. Engine availability, installation, maintenance and customer cost remained substantial constraints.${cadE}</p><p>By the middle 1960s its turbine twins competed with Beech's executive turboprops. These aircraft carried a different compromise from the Jet Mite: a useful cabin and regional business travel took priority over a very small owner's sports jet.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"market",title:"Industrial position",html:`<p>The continuing specialist market gave Aero Commander a place through the later expansion of American aviation. During the crash the smaller utility and business-aircraft firms were potential recipients of equipment and staff released by larger manufacturers. The source does not assign Aero Commander a particular purchase or merger, and no specific buyer or closing date follows from that opportunity.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"beechcraft.html",label:"Beechcraft",kicker:"Executive turboprop competitor"},{href:"general-aviation-manufacturers.html",label:"General-Aviation Manufacturers",kicker:"Manufacturing field"}],facts:[["Principal configuration","High-wing twin"],["Piston families","520 · 560"],["Markets","Executive · regional utility"],["Turbine direction","Compact turboprop twin"],["Exact later ownership","Open"]]
  }),
  "mooney-beech-aircraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Mooney–Beech Aircraft",eyebrow:"United States · combined aircraft concern · from 1991",turns:"101–104,110,114,118–120,126",
    lead:`Mooney and Beech combined in 1991 after the Open Door Depression. They retained the Mooney and Beech marques, with Mooney's personal-performance aircraft and Beech's premium business-aircraft range continuing under a common organization.${cadA}`,
    canon:"The merger year and retained marques are direct author direction. Mooney-Beech Aircraft Corporation is a working holding-company name. Shared facilities and finance arrangements are incorporated development; share percentages and later complete Frontier control remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"formation",title:"Formation",html:`<p>Both manufacturers survived the first crash year with reduced resources. Frontier held minority stakes in the two businesses, but neither had disappeared as a design organization. Their financial position led them to combine in 1991.${cadA}</p><p>The holding-company arrangement converted the earlier Frontier interests into a substantial minority stake. The percentage, share classes and full governing instrument are not specified. A minority investment did not give Frontier ownership of every Mooney and Beech program.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"organization",title:"Organization and marques",html:`<p>The combined concern shared purchasing, finance, certification staff, avionics procurement, composite expertise and dealer support. Mooney continued to emphasize low drag and owner-flown performance; Beech retained cabin, equipment and executive-aircraft priorities. Separate badges appeared together at the 1992 exposition.${cadE}</p><p>The combination did not make the earlier Jet Mite a Beech design or retroactively put Starship inside Mooney. The two earlier product histories remained identifiable within the merged business.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"development",title:"Joint development",html:`<p>A six-seat pressurized touring demonstrator belongs to the 1997 development program. It paired Beech cabin and pressurization experience with Mooney structures and drag reduction. The engine choice remained open between a turboprop and a high-performance piston installation; the account does not establish two production aircraft or a certified successor to Starship.${cadE}</p><p>Later owner-aircraft automation studies included monitoring and emergency recovery. A proposed early-2000s automated return system did not by itself establish a named production model, an approval authority, or universal landing capability.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"mooney-aircraft.html",label:"Mooney Aircraft",kicker:"Predecessor marque"},{href:"beechcraft.html",label:"Beechcraft",kicker:"Predecessor marque"},{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"Joint demonstrator"}],facts:[["Combination","1991"],["Marques","Mooney · Beech"],["Working corporate name","Mooney-Beech Aircraft Corporation"],["Frontier relationship","Minority investment; exact percentage open"],["Shared work","Finance · procurement · support · engineering"],["Later touring demonstrator","Six-seat pressurized development · 1997 development sequence"]]
  }),
  "general-aviation-manufacturers":cadArticle({
    title:"General-Aviation Manufacturers in the United States",eyebrow:"United States · light-aircraft manufacturing and operating support · 1941–2012",turns:"001–014,020–030,034–044,052–078,094–104,118–130",
    lead:`American general aviation developed through several manufacturers serving training, owner-pilots, farms, ranches, business and specialist utility work. Small rural airfields supported personal transport in the western states alongside a continuing passenger railway network. Gradual civilian demand, rather than a large pool of demobilized wartime pilots and aircraft, sustained the industry.${cadA}`,
    canon:"The broad ecology and comparatively lighter early regulatory pressure are author-established. Specific alternate company survival mechanisms and model dates are incorporated development. The existence or absence of a later FAA counterpart, exact equity schedules and optional company revivals remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"market",title:"Production and operating support",html:`<p>Affordable aircraft required repeatable airframe production, dependable engines, finance and a local operating network. Continental and Lycoming supplied a major part of the piston-engine base. Dealers, instructors, mechanics, parts stores, fuel and usable fields connected production to ownership. Cheap fuel alone did not remove insurance, overhaul, training or weather costs.${cadE}</p><p>Ranch and town aircraft complemented pickup trucks and railways. They served scattered destinations and regional journeys, while rail remained economical for many intercity trips. Aircraft ownership could mean a private machine, a shared aircraft, rental or a club arrangement; the record does not establish universal household ownership.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"manufacturers",title:"Manufacturers",html:cadTable(["Manufacturer","Principal lines and market","Development"],[[cadLink("cessna","Cessna"),"Training, family, rough-field, twin and business aircraft","Broad product ladder; later reduced around retained jet and support work."],[cadLink("piper-aircraft","Piper"),"Affordable training and personal aircraft; rural utility","Cub through cabin and low-wing aircraft; indigenous twin separate from Stinson."],[cadLink("beechcraft","Beechcraft"),"Premium owner-pilot and executive aircraft","Bonanza, twins, turboprops and Starship; 1991 combination with Mooney."],[cadLink("mooney-aircraft","Mooney"),"Efficient touring singles and personal jets","M20 and Jet Mite developments; retained marque after the Beech combination."],[cadLink("stinson-aircraft","Stinson"),"Comfortable cabin, cargo and utility aircraft","108, Station Wagon, Twin Stinson, Twin Wagon and Jet Wagon."],[cadLink("aero-commander","Aero Commander"),"High-wing corporate and utility twins","Piston twins followed by compact turbine installations."],["Aeronca","Champ, Chief and Sedan families; low-cost training and light utility","Remained toward the lighter end instead of duplicating the entire Cessna range."],["Taylorcraft","Economical light and rough-field aircraft; four-place development","Continued a modest-power utility school; detailed financing history remains unassigned."],["Luscombe","Silvaire and lightweight all-metal personal aircraft","Sporting handling and efficient construction; no required rush into the same four-seat market."],["ERCO","Ercoupe easy-flying lineage, then canard and composite experiments","Control simplicity and stall-limiting design; later jet-canard and joined-wing work."],[cadLink("frontier-aircraft","Frontier Aircraft"),"Folding-wing rough-field aircraft; later conventional production","Acquired selected post-crash capacity; civil-aircraft manufacture remains distinct from the orbital carrier."]].map(r=>[r[0],r[1],r[2]+cadE]))},
      {id:"specialists",title:"Smaller companies",html:`<p>Aeronca retained simple trainers and light cabin aircraft. Nosewheel development addressed new civilian pupils while keeping cost below heavier family aircraft. Taylorcraft's four-place project extended its light rough-field market. Luscombe continued an all-metal sporting identity rather than entering every heavier class at once.${cadE}</p><p>ERCO began with the Ercoupe's handling and control philosophy. Larger derivatives and the Model 62 canard tested whether easier handling could survive an increase in capacity. Burt Rutan later joined the ERCO development environment and contributed to composite and unconventional-aircraft work. He did not originate every earlier ERCO canard project.${cadE}</p><p>Globe's survival was an optional possibility in the discussion. No completed acquisition, continuous product line or independent survival through the later period is established for it.</p>`},
      {id:"helicopters",title:"Helicopter manufacturers",html:`<p>Bell developed the light-helicopter branch, Sikorsky larger transport rotorcraft, Piasecki/Vertol tandem-rotor machines, and Hiller light utility designs. Philippine operations enlarged the trained-pilot and maintenance constituency. Utility, surveying, oilfield and other specialized work followed; helicopters remained mechanically intensive and expensive beside ordinary fixed-wing aircraft.${cadE}</p><p>Turbine helicopters entered the later-1950s and 1960 development discussion. Specific civil model certificates, operators and service dates are not established by the presence of a military trial or historical comparison.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"oversight",title:"Civil oversight",html:`<p>Pilot certification, airworthiness inspection and controlled-airspace practice continued from the pre-divergence civil system. Airline operation required more extensive oversight than private flights from local fields. Growth brought navigation, weather, communications and airport investment.${cadE}</p><p>Operators supplied qualified crews, maintained their aircraft and planned dispatch. Airport authorities managed ground access and facilities; state and local rules could affect fields and noise. Civil inspection and airworthiness decisions remained separate from manufacturer testing, airline commercial permissions and military procurement. The later distribution of these powers and the civil authority's formal title are not fixed by this account.${cadE}</p><p>The military Aerospace Command, created in 1958, and independent Aerospace Force of 1962 had separate purposes from civil-airline regulation. No NASA existed. Manufacturer research, civil airworthiness decisions, accident investigation and military science contracts should be identified by their own functions; a military research institution did not certify its contractor's passenger aircraft.${cadA}</p>`},
      {id:"1990s",title:"Restructuring and later markets",html:`<p>The 1990 crash released manufacturing assets, tooling and engineering staff. Frontier expanded its conventional-aircraft capacity. Cessna and Piper continued as reduced manufacturers.${cadE} Beech and Mooney combined in 1991.${cadA} Smaller specialists retained their own market identities.${cadE}</p><p>Late-century expositions separated current products, prototypes and projected aircraft. Tiny jets, kit aircraft, conventional business jets and personal vertical-lift studies served different uses and carried different training and approval requirements. Later automatic-control demonstrations did not establish an aircraft that could fly safely in every weather or an entirely unpiloted national network.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"american-civil-aviation.html",label:"Civil Aviation in the United States",kicker:"National system"},{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"Development register"},{href:"personal-jet-aircraft.html",label:"Personal Jet Aircraft",kicker:"Jet programs"},{href:"travel-and-mobility-in-2000.html",label:"Travel and Mobility in 2000",kicker:"Transport ecology"}],facts:[["Demand pattern","Gradual civilian growth"],["Western role","Rural and regional personal transport"],["Support network","Fields · mechanics · instructors · dealers"],["Engine suppliers","Continental · Lycoming"],["Civil controls","Pilot and aircraft certification continued"],["1991 combination","Mooney–Beech"],["National regulator's later title","Not fixed"]]
  }),
  "experimental-light-aircraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Experimental Light Aircraft in the United States",eyebrow:"United States · civil demonstrators, prototypes and record aircraft · 1962–2003",turns:"041–046,052–056,068–072,078–084,094–100,114–118,125–126",
    lead:`American light-aircraft experiments tested compact turbines, canards, flying wings, twin-boom utility layouts, composites and automatic control. The 1962 programs were several distinct prototypes rather than a single simultaneous production launch. Later demonstrations and expositions kept experimental aircraft alongside the industry's ordinary piston and turbine ranges.${cadE}`,
    canon:"These are incorporated transcript developments. Dates denote the specified test, study or demonstration stage, not automatic certification or delivery. Unselected names, exact specifications, route rights and production totals remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"1962",title:"The 1962 programs",html:cadTable(["Developer and aircraft","Configuration and purpose","Milestone"],[[cadLink("mooney-aircraft","Mooney MJ-1 Jet Mite"),"Very small personal turbojet; owner-flown performance","Late-1962 first-flight development; range and baggage limits remained."],["ERCO Model 62 Canard Coupe","Canard experimental airframe pursuing easier handling","Late-1962 testing; not a confirmed four-seat production delivery."],["Northrop N-62","Small flying-wing demonstrator","Control and handling research; separate from the large passenger wing."],[cadLink("stinson-aircraft","Stinson Twin Wagon"),"Twin-boom utility aircraft with central cabin","1962 prototype phase; later middle-1960s production development."],[cadLink("beechcraft","Beech Model 87"),"Turboprop powerplant test installation","Experimental turbine work preceding a broader executive range."]].map(r=>[r[0],r[1],r[2]+cadE]))},
      {id:"controls",title:"Canards, wings and control systems",html:`<p>ERCO's canard work sought manageable low-speed behavior and stall characteristics while increasing capacity beyond the original Ercoupe. The Jet Canard demonstrator followed in the 1966 sequence, with further development in 1967. Burt Rutan joined the engineering work after the initial canard concept and contributed to composite construction and later layouts.${cadE}</p><p>Northrop's small wing supplied experience in stability, control and an unconventional cabin arrangement. It was not the same airframe as the full-scale passenger wing. The latter's 1962 control and structural-oscillation setback delayed that program while Gotha made its 1963 public demonstration.${cadE}</p><p>An ERCO/Wolkovitch joined-wing demonstrator appeared in the 1980 development sequence. It explored structural and aerodynamic alternatives rather than proving that every earlier canard design had become obsolete.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"utility",title:"Utility and small-jet experiments",html:`<p>Stinson's Jet Wagon applied the twin-boom utility idea to a six-seat jet. Cessna's high-wing owner jet explored regional-airport use, with study and display in 1973 followed by a 1974 first flight in the development chronology. Both remained subject to the economics of useful load, field length, fuel, maintenance and competing piston aircraft.${cadE}</p><p>Republic entered the civil light-aircraft speed competition in the later 1970s. The demonstration class had to be specified: a military-derived high-speed prototype did not become the fastest aircraft of every civil category or an ordinary owner-pilot transport merely by receiving a civil display role.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"composites",title:"Composite and endurance work",html:`<p>A Voyager-type round-the-world project in 1986 involved Dick Rutan and Jeana Yeager with an independent record team adjacent to the ERCO composite-development environment. The record team and ERCO remained distinct organizations.${cadE}</p><p>Beech's Starship demonstrated a composite, canard and pusher-turboprop executive aircraft during the same decade. A later Mooney–Beech six-seat touring demonstrator was a different, more conventional project combining the two firms' expertise. The 1997 demonstration did not settle an engine choice or a production certificate.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"automation",title:"Monitoring and automatic recovery",html:`<p>Personal-aircraft exhibits around 1999 included tiny jets, unconventional control and personal-lift projects. Aircraft shown as a rendering, mockup or flying prototype retained that status. Early-2000s work investigated more capable autopilots, pilot monitoring and emergency return assistance. A named manufacturer, certifier or universal operational approval is not established merely by the proposed capability.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"personal-jet-aircraft.html",label:"Personal Jet Aircraft",kicker:"Turbine development"},{href:"general-aviation-manufacturers.html",label:"General-Aviation Manufacturers",kicker:"Developers"},{href:"gotha-weltblick.html",label:"Gotha Weltblick",kicker:"Separate large transport"}],facts:[["1962 programs","MJ-1 · Model 62 · N-62 · Twin Wagon · Model 87"],["1966 development","ERCO Jet Canard"],["1980 development","ERCO/Wolkovitch joined wing"],["1986 project","Voyager-type endurance aircraft"],["Status distinction","Study · demonstrator · certificate · production"],["Unsupported transfer","No automatic NASA sponsorship or historical company genealogy"]]
  }),
  "personal-jet-aircraft":cadArticle({
    title:"Personal Jet Aircraft in the United States",eyebrow:"United States · owner-flown, utility and small business jets · from 1962",turns:"041–046,048,052–072,077–084,098–104,110–118",
    lead:`American personal-jet development divided into owner-flown sporting aircraft, small business transports and utility jets. Mooney's tiny Jet Mite, Stinson's cargo-oriented Jet Wagon, ERCO's canard and Cessna's Citation answered different requirements. Turboprops and piston aircraft continued beside them.${cadE}`,
    canon:"The tiny owner-jet direction is author-established; individual model names and dates are incorporated developments. Program tables identify development stages and do not supply universal service dates or production certificates.",
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early development",html:`<p>The smallest jet proposal concerned a two-to-four-place aircraft, rather than a shortened corporate transport. Mooney flew the MJ-1 Jet Mite in the late-1962 development sequence. Initial orders and a refined early batch followed before a larger four-seat design. The engine, fuel load and qualification required for a turbine could remain costly even in a small airframe.${cadE}</p><p>The larger aircraft manufacturers pursued a business jet with an equipped cabin and more payload. Their customers, maintenance schedules and financing differed from those of the individual sporting owner. The two markets could share engine or avionics suppliers without becoming the same class of aircraft.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"programs",title:"Program register",html:cadTable(["Program","Purpose","Development sequence"],[[cadLink("mooney-aircraft","MJ-1 and later Mooney jets"),"Small owner-flown performance aircraft","Late-1962 demonstrator; improved early batch, then middle-1960s four-seat prototype and range development."],[cadLink("stinson-aircraft","Stinson Jet Wagon"),"Six-seat passenger/cargo utility jet","1966 study; 1967 flying development; later supervised-freight study rather than assumed uncrewed service."],["ERCO Jet Canard","Canard layout with compact turbine","1966 demonstrator; subsequent control, composite and handling work."],[cadLink("cessna","Cessna FanJet/Citation"),"Conventional practical small business jet","1969 prototype; 1971 certification in the development sequence; subsequent delivery and support expansion."],["Cessna Model 515 Skyjet","High-wing regional owner jet","1973 study/exposition project; 1974 first-flight stage; continued economic evaluation."],["Northrop small wing","Range and efficiency through unconventional geometry","Small wing and control development; distinct from Northrop's airliner."],["Republic civil high-speed aircraft","Record and high-speed private-aircraft development","Late-1970s demonstration competition; performance class and production status require separate qualification."],["Frontier light business jet","Regional six-to-eight-seat demonstrator","Later-1990s development using expanded civil manufacturing capacity."]].map(r=>[r[0],r[1],r[2]+cadE]))},
      {id:"operation",title:"Operating limits",html:`<p>Small jets needed suitable runways, fuel, maintenance and weather information. A high engine installation or short landing gear could improve access and loading, but did not give a jet the strip tolerance of a Cub. The utility piston twin could retain a lower cost for short flights with awkward loads.${cadE}</p><p>Owner-flown speed brought training, insurance and endurance constraints. Corporate operators valued predictable servicing, cabin space and dealer coverage. Afterburning or record flights demonstrated a different operating case from routine travel with passengers and baggage.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"later",title:"Later competition",html:`<p>Foreign utility and fast civil aircraft entered American comparisons during the later exhibition and reciprocal-trade periods. Mooney pursued greater speed; Cessna continued practical jets and later the Citation X; Stinson retained utility aircraft. The 1990 crash reduced several firms and enlarged Frontier's conventional production capacity.${cadE} Mooney and Beech combined in 1991.${cadA}</p><p>Personal-flight exhibits around the millennium again included microjets and experimental lift systems. An exhibit did not show that a design had reached training fleets, unrestricted commercial operation or an automobile-like ownership cost.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"Demonstrator register"},{href:"general-aviation-manufacturers.html",label:"General-Aviation Manufacturers",kicker:"Manufacturing"},{href:"supersonic-passenger-aircraft.html",label:"Supersonic Passenger Aircraft",kicker:"Separate large transport class"}],facts:[["Earliest small-jet development","MJ-1 Jet Mite · late 1962"],["Utility jet","Stinson Jet Wagon"],["Conventional business jet","Cessna Citation"],["Other configurations","Canard · flying wing · high-wing jet"],["Continuing alternatives","Piston singles/twins · turboprops"],["Record claims","Aircraft class and operating conditions required"]]
  }),
  "concorde":cadArticle({
    title:"Concorde",eyebrow:"Anglo-American supersonic passenger aircraft · first flight 1969",turns:"047–048,052,056–062,127–128",
    lead:`Concorde is the supersonic passenger aircraft developed jointly by British Aircraft Corporation and Convair. It first flew in 1969 before Germany's Arado P.1100. The German aircraft entered paying passenger service first, while disputes within the Anglo-American partnership delayed Concorde's commercial entry.${cadA}`,
    canon:"The partners, name, 1969 first flight and order of first flight versus paying service are author-established. Initial approach in 1964 and agreement around 1965 are incorporated chronology. Exact first flight day, initial route, operator, fare-service date, production division and certificate are open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"agreement",title:"Agreement and workshare",html:`<p>Convair approached BAC during 1964, with agreement following around 1965 in the development program sequence. BAC contributed British civil-aircraft, high-speed and certification experience; Convair supplied American high-speed-aircraft engineering and access to a major potential market. Exact equity, engine partners, production shares and contractual rights are not specified.${cadE}</p><p>The joint aircraft became Concorde. Its development was an Anglo-American program. The agreement's exact legal form and production title remained separate from the identity of the two developers.${cadA}</p>`},
      {id:"flight",title:"First flight and trials",html:`<p>Concorde flew in 1969, ahead of Arado's P.1100 later that year.${cadA} Transatlantic demonstrations belonged to the publicity and trials program. A reported London–New York appearance did not make a prototype maiden flight, a supersonic crossing and the first fare-paying timetable the same event.</p><p>Flight trials had to establish performance, structure, handling, engine operation and airport compatibility. Certification, production responsibility and customer support remained subjects of disagreement between the partners. The aircraft's first-flight priority survived the delay to paying operation.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"service",title:"Passenger service",html:`<p>Arado reached paying service before Concorde.${cadA} The broad sequence places the German entry in the earlier 1970s and Concorde's later in that decade's first half. Exact service-entry days and initial operators remain unspecified.</p><p>Long overwater routes, particularly across the Atlantic, offered a stronger commercial case than short inland flights. High fuel use, specialized maintenance, noise, compatible airports and premium fares constrained the network. Commercial entry did not imply a right to fly supersonically above every inhabited territory.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"convair",title:"Convair's subsequent program",html:`<p>Convair's experience of the joint program led it to pursue a wholly independent transport. The later variable-sweep aircraft was its own engineering project. It did not inherit a NASA study or become a second BAC–Convair partnership. Low-boom work later addressed the limits imposed on inland operation.${cadA}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"british-aircraft-corporation.html",label:"British Aircraft Corporation",kicker:"British partner"},{href:"convair.html",label:"Convair",kicker:"American partner"},{href:"arado-p1100.html",label:"Arado P.1100",kicker:"First paying service"},{href:"convair-supersonic-transports.html",label:"Convair Supersonic Transports",kicker:"Independent follow-on"}],facts:[["Developers","BAC · Convair"],["Working approach/agreement","1964 / around 1965"],["First flight","1969"],["First-flight priority","Before Arado P.1100"],["Paying-service priority","Arado before Concorde"],["Principal commercial geography","Long overwater routes"],["Exact first-service date and operator","Open"]],categories:["Concorde","Supersonic transports","British civil aircraft","American civil aircraft"]
  }),
  "arado-p1100":cadArticle({
    title:"Arado P.1100",eyebrow:"Germany · supersonic passenger aircraft · first flight 1969",turns:"047–048,052,056–062,127–128",
    lead:`The Arado P.1100 is Germany's supersonic passenger-aircraft program. Its first flight followed BAC–Convair Concorde in 1969, but it reached paying passenger service before the Anglo-American aircraft.${cadA}`,
    canon:"Arado's program name, 1969 first-flight order and priority in paying service are author-established. Exact first-service year, operator, aircraft capacity, propulsion, production totals and route list remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"development",title:"Development",html:`<p>P.1100 developed within the German high-speed-aircraft industry as the Anglo-American Concorde partnership assembled its own program. By 1968 American industrial reporting treated the German aircraft as a serious approaching competitor. The program could coordinate design and commercial preparation within a more unified national industrial setting than the divided BAC–Convair partnership.${cadE}</p><p>The program's Arado identity does not alone settle the later company's equity or its relation to other German design groups. A project number supplies neither a complete corporate succession nor an engine specification.</p>`},
      {id:"milestones",title:"Flight and commercial milestones",html:`${cadTable(["Milestone","Record"],[["First flight","1969, after Concorde's first flight."+cadA],["Paying service","Before Concorde; the exact date and first operator remain open."+cadA],["Working chronology","The German commercial entry preceded the Anglo-American entry; no exact service-entry day is fixed."]])}<p>Publicity could claim the commercial first even though the first-flight record belonged to Concorde. A prototype appearance did not establish scheduled ticketed operation, and a national announcement did not identify every subsequent route approval.</p>`},
      {id:"operations",title:"Operating requirements",html:`<p>The aircraft shared the large supersonic transport class's requirements for suitable runways, specialized maintenance, high-temperature structures and a market willing to pay for speed. Sonic booms and airport noise limited routes even where the state supported development. Premium long-distance flights offered a better prospect than brief sectors with little time at supersonic cruise.${cadE}</p><p>No full production specification or complete fleet history is established. The P.1100's service priority should not be expanded into an unsupported global traffic share, accident record or retirement date.</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"arado.html",label:"Arado",kicker:"Design organization"},{href:"concorde.html",label:"Concorde",kicker:"First-flight competitor"},{href:"german-civil-aviation.html",label:"Civil Aviation in Germany",kicker:"Operating system"},{href:"supersonic-passenger-aircraft.html",label:"Supersonic Passenger Aircraft",kicker:"Program comparison"}],facts:[["Program","Arado P.1100"],["Country","Germany"],["First flight","1969; after Concorde"],["Commercial priority","First of the two into paying service"],["Exact commercial date","Open"],["Detailed specification and fleet","Open"]],categories:["Arado","German civil aircraft","Supersonic transports"]
  }),
  "convair-supersonic-transports":cadArticle({
    title:"Convair Supersonic Transports",eyebrow:"United States · independent variable-sweep transport and low-boom development · 1970s–2005",turns:"059–062,084–090,094–100,127–130",
    lead:`After its work with BAC on Concorde, Convair developed an independent supersonic transport with a variable-sweep planform. It publicly presented the aircraft in 1983. Restrictions on inland sonic booms led the company to pursue quieter supersonic aircraft as well as route permissions.${cadA}`,
    canon:"Independent Convair authorship, the variable-sweep design direction, 1983 public presentation and the response to overflight restrictions are author-established. SCAT-16 is an out-of-world geometry reference, not an in-world NASA project. Exact first flight, certification, paying-service and quiet-successor dates remain unsettled.",
    sections:[
      {id:"origin",title:"Independent development",html:`<p>Convair began the independent program after the disputes surrounding Concorde. It retained experience of high-speed structures, propulsion integration and the compromise between airport operation and supersonic cruise. A variable-sweep wing offered a separate design solution rather than a second joint Concorde airframe.${cadA}</p><p>Early-1970s internal work is part of the incorporated chronology. Fixed-wing and movable-wing possibilities appeared during development, but the selected variable-sweep architecture remained Convair's own project. ERCO canard and low-speed-control expertise was a possible bounded research contribution, not ownership of half the transport.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"milestones",title:"Development milestones",html:cadTable(["Stage","Record and limits"],[["Internal design","Early 1970s development; an exact program authorization date is not fixed."+cadE],["Private flight and trials","Later-1970s prototype and around-1980 trials belong to the development sequence; no public passenger timetable follows from them."+cadE],["Near-production testing","Early-1980s proving work and certification preparation are distinct from public unveiling."+cadE],["Public presentation","1983. Earlier public-rollout descriptions yield to this date."+cadA],["Paying service","After the public presentation; exact first fare and route approval remain open."],["Low-boom work","Began in the 1983 period; later research and full-aircraft demonstrations are separate stages."+cadA]])},
      {id:"routes",title:"Routes and noise",html:`<p>Long overwater sectors reduced the population exposed to the boom. Inland routes crossed jurisdictions with differing tolerances and airport constraints. Federal enthusiasm for supersonic flight did not remove every dispute about noise, operating hours and local effects.${cadA}</p><p>Convair sought negotiated route access and lobbied against blanket prohibitions while working on boom reduction. A flight could be authorized under defined conditions rather than establishing nationwide permission. Certification of airworthiness and permission to use a route answered different questions.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"quiet",title:"Low-boom development",html:`<p>The quiet-aircraft program examined shaping and the pressure signature reaching the ground. A 1989 research demonstrator and a late-1990s larger civil demonstrator appear in the development chronology. Their precise airframes and first-flight dates remain unsettled. They are distinct possible development stages rather than two conflicting claims to the first airline aircraft.${cadE}</p><p>The crash interrupted investment. Later work continued toward production prototypes and limited practical service in the early 2000s development sequence. The proposed 2003–04 window identifies a development placement, not a fixed first ticket sale. Quiet operation meant a reduced and managed signature, not an inaudible aircraft.${cadE}</p>`},
      {id:"2005",title:"Operations and competition by 2005",html:`<p>Long-distance and transoceanic routes remained the strongest case for an SST. Short sectors offered too little supersonic cruise time to recover the cost of climb, acceleration and specialized operation. High-speed rail and maglev competed for many downtown-to-downtown journeys while aircraft retained a stronger advantage on longer routes.${cadA}</p><p>Flight planning increasingly combined aircraft shape, operating altitude, speed, routing and weather. Atmospheric conditions could focus or weaken a pressure signature at the ground. Meteorological prediction and route selection therefore remained part of low-boom operations alongside airframe engineering.${cadE}</p>`}
    ],related:[{href:"convair.html",label:"Convair",kicker:"Developer"},{href:"concorde.html",label:"Concorde",kicker:"Earlier joint program"},{href:"supersonic-passenger-aircraft.html",label:"Supersonic Passenger Aircraft",kicker:"Comparison"},{href:"travel-and-mobility-in-2000.html",label:"Travel and Mobility in 2000",kicker:"Rail and air markets"}],facts:[["Developer","Convair independently"],["Planform","Variable sweep"],["Public presentation","1983"],["First paying-service date","Open"],["Low-boom program","From the 1983 period"],["Later demonstrations","Research article and larger civil demonstrator; exact dates open"],["In-world NASA","None"],["Principal market","Long-range routes with negotiated operating permissions"]],categories:["Convair","Supersonic transports","American civil aircraft","Aviation noise"]
  })
});

window.deepArticles["frontier-aircraft"]=cadArticle({
  title:"Frontier Aircraft",eyebrow:"United States · folding-wing bush aircraft, solar ultralights and conventional aviation",turns:"067–088,091–104,110–120",
  lead:`Frontier Aircraft is the American civil-aircraft business associated with folding-wing bush planes, utility aircraft and solar-assisted ultralights. Usually called Frontier, it used a Rocky Mountains and plains emblem. Larry Mauro joined its ultralight development work after demonstrating Solar Riser.${cadA} The concern broadened its conventional manufacturing business after the 1990 crash, using the Frontier Aviation identity.${cadE}`,
  canon:"The Frontier name and emblem, Mauro's recruitment, cautious financing and minority holdings in Mooney and Beech are author-established. Sawtooth was an earlier discarded drafting name, not a verified legal predecessor or corporate renaming. Trailwing is a working model name; exact foundation, headquarters, equity instruments, brand agreements and individual asset closing dates remain open.",
  sections:[
    {id:"formation",title:"Early aircraft business",html:`<p>The original business grew from the western light-aircraft and homebuilt environment. Its early work included plans, components, modifications and simple utility aircraft. By the 1973–74 development sequence it had a small folding-wing bush aircraft offered as the Trailwing. The exact foundation year and location are not fixed.${cadE}</p><p>Frontier's light aircraft targeted garage storage, trailering and rough-field use. The pilot shut down the aircraft, released structural locks and folded the wings aft alongside the fuselage. Folded storage width was separate from the space required to land or take off. A parking-space-sized stored aircraft did not imply permission to land among vehicles on a public street.${cadE}</p>`},
    {id:"range",title:"Folding-wing range",html:`<p>The early Trailwing school used two seats, a high wing, fixed tailwheel landing gear and light construction. Larger tires, stronger gear and cargo provisions supported the bush version. The later range included a simple recreational/training version and larger traveling or utility aircraft.${cadE}</p><p>The smallest machines could fold a complete wing rearward. Larger models used folding outer panels or tips while retaining a fixed center section. Increased size brought more difficult control connections, fuel arrangements and structural loads. Other manufacturers investigated their own systems; the field did not adopt one universal folding mechanism.${cadE}</p><p>Overseas utility and folding-wing aircraft supplied competitive benchmarks during the middle 1970s. Frontier refined useful load, field performance, maintenance cost and storage rather than claiming that its general folding-wing idea was unique.${cadE}</p>`},
    {id:"solar",title:"Larry Mauro and solar flight",html:`<p>Larry Mauro demonstrated Solar Riser and then joined Frontier to lead part of its ultralight and solar-flight work.${cadA} The demonstration belonged to the 1979 development sequence, followed by expanded company work around 1980.${cadE}</p><p>The solar program investigated lighter photovoltaic panels and wiring, low-drag rigging, reduced structural mass, efficient motor controls and energy buffering. A battery could supply takeoff and climb while sunlight contributed to cruise and replenishment. The company sought a usable product, rather than merely fitting panels to an otherwise unchanged ultralight.${cadE}</p><p>A purpose-built solar-ultralight prototype and later early-1980s customer development appear in the development chronology. Customer machines were solar-assisted, with important payload, weather and endurance limits. Low gasoline prices weakened the fuel-saving case; recreation and demonstration remained substantial parts of the market.${cadE}</p>`},
    {id:"organization",title:"The Frontier businesses",html:`<p>The civil-aircraft operation and Voss's <a href='frontier-orbital-lines.html'>Frontier Orbital Lines</a> carried the same Frontier brand but performed different work. The orbital operating concern dated from 1985; its associated engineering activity was commonly called Frontier Spaceplanes. A bush-aircraft sale was not an orbital passenger contract, and neither name alone identifies the exact legal ownership of the other business.${cadE}</p><p>Brand and engineering connections allowed the businesses to exchange publicity and some structural knowledge. The civil operation remained cautious about expansion, improving aircraft, folding mechanisms and corrosion protection while reducing debt. Its financing priorities differed from those of the capital-intensive spacecraft development programs.${cadE}</p>`},
    {id:"crash",title:"The crash and Frontier Aviation",html:`<p>Frontier's aircraft managers directed extraordinary receipts toward debt reduction as speculation increased before the 1990 crash.${cadA} The civil business acquired selected former Cessna and Piper manufacturing capacity and tooling, together with staff and support infrastructure. Cessna and Piper continued as smaller manufacturers rather than disappearing into the Frontier name.${cadE}</p><p>Frontier held minority interests in both Mooney and Beech when the two manufacturers combined in 1991.${cadA} The merged arrangement carried those interests into the combined concern. The stake percentage and later control remain unspecified; a minority holding did not establish outright ownership of either marque.${cadE}</p><p>Frontier Aviation became the broader civil-business identity as conventional aircraft joined the bush and ultralight lines. A straightforward four-seat touring aircraft appeared in the 1992 exposition. It emphasized cabin comfort, repeatable manufacture, accessible service and moderate cost. Later development included a regional six-to-eight-seat light-jet demonstrator; its exhibition did not itself establish a production certificate.${cadE}</p>`}
  ],related:[{href:"general-aviation-manufacturers.html",label:"General-Aviation Manufacturers",kicker:"Industry"},{href:"mooney-beech-aircraft.html",label:"Mooney–Beech Aircraft",kicker:"Minority investment"},{href:"frontier-orbital-lines.html",label:"Frontier Orbital Lines",kicker:"Distinct orbital operator"},{href:"experimental-light-aircraft.html",label:"Experimental Light Aircraft",kicker:"Solar and prototype work"}],facts:[["Usual name","Frontier"],["Emblem","Rocky Mountains and plains"],["Early specialty","Folding-wing bush and utility aircraft"],["Working early model","Trailwing"],["Solar/ultralight developer","Larry Mauro"],["Later civil identity","Frontier Aviation"],["Orbital operator","Frontier Orbital Lines; distinct function"],["1991 interests","Minority stakes in Mooney and Beech"],["Exact foundation and ownership schedule","Open"]]
});
